Task 1: Install Ansible and Vault, Then Deploy Vault with a Playbook
Subtask 1.1: Install Ansible
pip3 install --user ansible

echo 'export PATH=$PATH:~/.local/bin' >> ~/.bashrc
export PATH=$PATH:~/.local/bin

ansible --version
Expected result: ansible --version prints the Ansible core version (for example ansible [core 2.x.x]) along with the Python interpreter path.

Subtask 1.2: Install HashiCorp Vault
Use the official HashiCorp APT repository so the install stays correct regardless of the exact release version.

wget -O- https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg

echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg] https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com $(lsb_release -cs) main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y vault

vault version
Expected result: vault version prints something like Vault v1.x.x. If your distribution codename is not supported by the HashiCorp repo, download the matching zip for your architecture from releases.hashicorp.com/vault instead and place the binary in /usr/local/bin/vault.

Subtask 1.3: Create the Project Directory Structure
mkdir -p ~/vault-ansible-lab/{playbooks,inventory,roles/vault/{tasks,templates,defaults,handlers}}
cd ~/vault-ansible-lab

cat > inventory/hosts << 'EOF'
[vault_servers]
localhost ansible_connection=local

[vault_servers:vars]
vault_port=8200
vault_address=127.0.0.1
EOF
Deliverable: running cat ~/vault-ansible-lab/inventory/hosts shows the inventory content above.

Subtask 1.4: Create the Vault Role (Config, Service, Defaults, Handlers)
cat > ~/vault-ansible-lab/roles/vault/defaults/main.yml << 'EOF'
---
vault_port: 8200
vault_address: "127.0.0.1"
vault_user: "vault"
vault_group: "vault"
vault_config_dir: "/etc/vault.d"
vault_data_dir: "/opt/vault/data"
EOF

cat > ~/vault-ansible-lab/roles/vault/templates/vault.hcl.j2 << 'EOF'
ui = true
disable_mlock = true

storage "file" {
  path = "{{ vault_data_dir }}"
}

listener "tcp" {
  address     = "{{ vault_address }}:{{ vault_port }}"
  tls_disable = true
}

api_addr = "http://{{ vault_address }}:{{ vault_port }}"
EOF

cat > ~/vault-ansible-lab/roles/vault/templates/vault.service.j2 << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=HashiCorp Vault
Documentation=https://www.vaultproject.io/docs/
Requires=network-online.target
After=network-online.target
ConditionFileNotEmpty={{ vault_config_dir }}/vault.hcl

[Service]
Type=notify
User={{ vault_user }}
Group={{ vault_group }}
ExecStart=/usr/bin/vault server -config={{ vault_config_dir }}/vault.hcl
ExecReload=/bin/kill -HUP $MAINPID
KillMode=process
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
LimitNOFILE=65536
LimitMEMLOCK=infinity

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

cat > ~/vault-ansible-lab/roles/vault/handlers/main.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: reload systemd
  systemd:
    daemon_reload: yes
  become: yes

- name: restart vault
  systemd:
    name: vault
    state: restarted
  become: yes
EOF

cat > ~/vault-ansible-lab/roles/vault/tasks/main.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Create vault group
  group:
    name: "{{ vault_group }}"
    system: yes
  become: yes

- name: Create vault user
  user:
    name: "{{ vault_user }}"
    group: "{{ vault_group }}"
    system: yes
    shell: /usr/sbin/nologin
    createhome: no
  become: yes

- name: Create vault directories
  file:
    path: "{{ item }}"
    state: directory
    owner: "{{ vault_user }}"
    group: "{{ vault_group }}"
    mode: '0750'
  loop:
    - "{{ vault_config_dir }}"
    - "{{ vault_data_dir }}"
  become: yes

- name: Generate vault configuration
  template:
    src: vault.hcl.j2
    dest: "{{ vault_config_dir }}/vault.hcl"
    owner: "{{ vault_user }}"
    group: "{{ vault_group }}"
    mode: '0640'
  become: yes
  notify: restart vault

- name: Create vault systemd service
  template:
    src: vault.service.j2
    dest: /etc/systemd/system/vault.service
    owner: root
    group: root
    mode: '0644'
  become: yes
  notify:
    - reload systemd
    - restart vault

- name: Start and enable vault service
  systemd:
    name: vault
    state: started
    enabled: yes
    daemon_reload: yes
  become: yes

- name: Wait for vault API to respond
  uri:
    url: "http://{{ vault_address }}:{{ vault_port }}/v1/sys/health"
    method: GET
    status_code: [200, 429, 472, 473, 501, 503]
  register: vault_health_check
  until: vault_health_check.status is defined
  retries: 10
  delay: 3
EOF
Subtask 1.5: Create and Run the Deployment Playbook
cat > ~/vault-ansible-lab/playbooks/deploy-vault.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Deploy HashiCorp Vault
  hosts: vault_servers
  become: yes
  roles:
    - vault

  post_tasks:
    - name: Show vault status
      command: vault status
      environment:
        VAULT_ADDR: "http://{{ vault_address }}:{{ vault_port }}"
      register: vault_status
      failed_when: false
      changed_when: false

    - name: Display vault status output
      debug:
        var: vault_status.stdout_lines
EOF

cd ~/vault-ansible-lab
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts playbooks/deploy-vault.yml
Deliverable and expected result: the playbook run ends with PLAY RECAP showing failed=0, and the debug task prints vault_status.stdout_lines including a line Sealed true (Vault is installed and running but not yet initialized/unsealed). Confirm independently:

sudo systemctl status vault --no-pager
export VAULT_ADDR='http://127.0.0.1:8200'
vault status
You should see active (running) in the systemd status and Vault's status table showing Initialized: false and Sealed: true.

Task 2: Automate Initialization, Policies, and Secrets with Ansible
Subtask 2.1: Initialize and Unseal Vault
cat > ~/vault-ansible-lab/playbooks/initialize-vault.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Initialize and Unseal Vault
  hosts: vault_servers
  gather_facts: no
  vars:
    vault_addr: "http://{{ vault_address }}:{{ vault_port }}"

  tasks:
    - name: Check vault init status
      uri:
        url: "{{ vault_addr }}/v1/sys/init"
        method: GET
      register: vault_init_status

    - name: Initialize vault with 5 key shares, threshold 3
      uri:
        url: "{{ vault_addr }}/v1/sys/init"
        method: PUT
        body_format: json
        body:
          secret_shares: 5
          secret_threshold: 3
        status_code: 200
      register: vault_init
      when: not vault_init_status.json.initialized

    - name: Save root token and unseal keys locally
      copy:
        content: |
          Root Token: {{ vault_init.json.root_token }}
          Unseal Key 1: {{ vault_init.json.keys[0] }}
          Unseal Key 2: {{ vault_init.json.keys[1] }}
          Unseal Key 3: {{ vault_init.json.keys[2] }}
          Unseal Key 4: {{ vault_init.json.keys[3] }}
          Unseal Key 5: {{ vault_init.json.keys[4] }}
        dest: "{{ lookup('env','HOME') }}/vault-keys.txt"
        mode: '0600'
      delegate_to: localhost
      when: vault_init is changed

    - name: Read existing keys file if vault was already initialized
      slurp:
        src: "{{ lookup('env','HOME') }}/vault-keys.txt"
      register: existing_keys_file
      delegate_to: localhost
      when: vault_init_status.json.initialized

    - name: Build unseal key list from a freshly initialized vault
      set_fact:
        vault_unseal_keys: "{{ vault_init.json.keys[:3] }}"
      when: vault_init is changed

    - name: Build unseal key list from an existing keys file
      set_fact:
        vault_unseal_keys: "{{ (existing_keys_file.content | b64decode).splitlines() | select('match', '^Unseal Key [123]:') | map('regex_replace', '^Unseal Key [123]: ', '') | list }}"
      when: vault_init_status.json.initialized and vault_unseal_keys is not defined

    - name: Unseal vault using three keys
      uri:
        url: "{{ vault_addr }}/v1/sys/unseal"
        method: PUT
        body_format: json
        body:
          key: "{{ item }}"
        status_code: 200
      loop: "{{ vault_unseal_keys }}"

    - name: Verify vault seal status
      uri:
        url: "{{ vault_addr }}/v1/sys/seal-status"
        method: GET
      register: vault_seal_status

    - name: Display seal status
      debug:
        msg: "Vault sealed: {{ vault_seal_status.json.sealed }}"
EOF

cd ~/vault-ansible-lab
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts playbooks/initialize-vault.yml
Deliverable: ~/vault-keys.txt exists with a root token and five unseal keys, and the playbook's final debug message reads Vault sealed: False. Confirm with:

cat ~/vault-keys.txt
export VAULT_ADDR='http://127.0.0.1:8200'
vault status
vault status should show Sealed: false and Initialized: true.

Subtask 2.2: Enable a Secrets Engine, Write Secrets, and Create Policies
cat > ~/vault-ansible-lab/playbooks/manage-vault-data.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Enable secrets engine, write secrets, create policies
  hosts: vault_servers
  gather_facts: no
  vars:
    vault_addr: "http://{{ vault_address }}:{{ vault_port }}"
    vault_token: "{{ lookup('file', lookup('env','HOME') + '/vault-keys.txt') | regex_search('Root Token: (\\S+)', '\\1') | first }}"

  tasks:
    - name: Enable KV version 2 secrets engine at secret/
      uri:
        url: "{{ vault_addr }}/v1/sys/mounts/secret"
        method: POST
        headers:
          X-Vault-Token: "{{ vault_token }}"
        body_format: json
        body:
          type: "kv"
          options:
            version: "2"
        status_code: [204, 400]

    - name: Write a sample database secret
      uri:
        url: "{{ vault_addr }}/v1/secret/data/database"
        method: POST
        headers:
          X-Vault-Token: "{{ vault_token }}"
        body_format: json
        body:
          data:
            username: "dbadmin"
            password: "lab-only-password-123"
            host: "db.lab.local"
            port: "5432"
        status_code: 200

    - name: Write a sample development secret
      uri:
        url: "{{ vault_addr }}/v1/secret/data/dev/app"
        method: POST
        headers:
          X-Vault-Token: "{{ vault_token }}"
        body_format: json
        body:
          data:
            debug_mode: "true"
            log_level: "debug"
        status_code: 200

    - name: Create a developer policy limited to secret/data/dev/*
      uri:
        url: "{{ vault_addr }}/v1/sys/policies/acl/developer"
        method: PUT
        headers:
          X-Vault-Token: "{{ vault_token }}"
        body_format: json
        body:
          policy: |
            path "secret/data/dev/*" {
              capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list"]
            }
            path "secret/metadata/dev/*" {
              capabilities = ["list", "read", "delete"]
            }
        status_code: 204

    - name: List configured policies
      uri:
        url: "{{ vault_addr }}/v1/sys/policies/acl"
        method: LIST
        headers:
          X-Vault-Token: "{{ vault_token }}"
        status_code: 200
      register: vault_policies

    - name: Display policy names
      debug:
        msg: "Configured policies: {{ vault_policies.json.data.keys }}"
EOF

cd ~/vault-ansible-lab
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts playbooks/manage-vault-data.yml
Deliverable: the final debug task prints Configured policies: including default, root, and developer. Confirm manually:

export VAULT_ADDR='http://127.0.0.1:8200'
export VAULT_TOKEN=$(grep "Root Token:" ~/vault-keys.txt | awk '{print $3}')
vault kv get secret/database
vault policy read developer
vault kv get secret/database should print the key-value pairs username, password, host, and port under a Data heading.

Subtask 2.3: Automate Secret Retrieval into a Consuming Application File
cat > ~/vault-ansible-lab/playbooks/retrieve-secrets.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Retrieve secrets and render an application config
  hosts: vault_servers
  gather_facts: no
  vars:
    vault_addr: "http://{{ vault_address }}:{{ vault_port }}"
    vault_token: "{{ lookup('file', lookup('env','HOME') + '/vault-keys.txt') | regex_search('Root Token: (\\S+)', '\\1') | first }}"

  tasks:
    - name: Read database secret
      uri:
        url: "{{ vault_addr }}/v1/secret/data/database"
        method: GET
        headers:
          X-Vault-Token: "{{ vault_token }}"
        status_code: 200
      register: db_secret

    - name: Render application configuration file with retrieved secret values
      copy:
        content: |
          # Generated by Ansible from Vault secret data - lab use only
          [database]
          host = {{ db_secret.json.data.data.host }}
          port = {{ db_secret.json.data.data.port }}
          username = {{ db_secret.json.data.data.username }}
          password = {{ db_secret.json.data.data.password }}
        dest: "{{ lookup('env','HOME') }}/app-config.ini"
        mode: '0600'
      delegate_to: localhost

    - name: Confirm file was written
      stat:
        path: "{{ lookup('env','HOME') }}/app-config.ini"
      delegate_to: localhost
      register: config_stat

    - name: Display confirmation
      debug:
        msg: "app-config.ini exists: {{ config_stat.stat.exists }}, size: {{ config_stat.stat.size }} bytes"
EOF

cd ~/vault-ansible-lab
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts playbooks/retrieve-secrets.yml
cat ~/app-config.ini
Deliverable: ~/app-config.ini contains a [database] section populated with the values written into Vault in Subtask 2.2, proving that the secret was retrieved and rendered automatically rather than hardcoded.

Task 3: Verify Policy Enforcement and Monitor Vault Health
Subtask 3.1: Verify Policy Enforcement with a Scoped Token
export VAULT_ADDR='http://127.0.0.1:8200'
export VAULT_TOKEN=$(grep "Root Token:" ~/vault-keys.txt | awk '{print $3}')

# Create a token restricted to the developer policy
DEV_TOKEN=$(vault token create -policy="developer" -field=token)
echo "Developer token: $DEV_TOKEN"

# As the developer token, access to secret/dev/app should succeed
VAULT_TOKEN=$DEV_TOKEN vault kv get secret/dev/app

# As the developer token, access to secret/database should be denied
VAULT_TOKEN=$DEV_TOKEN vault kv get secret/database
Expected result: the first vault kv get secret/dev/app command succeeds and prints the debug_mode and log_level keys. The second command against secret/database fails with Error making API request and a 403 permission denied message, confirming the developer policy correctly restricts access outside secret/data/dev/*.

Subtask 3.2: Performance and Monitoring Test
Vault exposes a health endpoint and, once enabled, a Prometheus-format telemetry endpoint. In this subtask you check both to confirm the deployment is healthy and observable.

# Check basic health and response time
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP status: %{http_code}, response time: %{time_total}s\n" \
  http://127.0.0.1:8200/v1/sys/health

# Inspect full health JSON
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8200/v1/sys/health | jq
Expected result: the health check returns HTTP status 200 (initialized, unsealed, active) with a response time well under one second, and the JSON body includes fields initialized: true, sealed: false, and standby: false.

Enable telemetry so Vault exposes metrics, then confirm the metrics endpoint responds:

export VAULT_TOKEN=$(grep "Root Token:" ~/vault-keys.txt | awk '{print $3}')

# Vault must be configured with telemetry to expose /v1/sys/metrics; check the endpoint
# using the root token (sys/metrics requires a token with sufficient permissions)
curl -s -H "X-Vault-Token: $VAULT_TOKEN" \
  http://127.0.0.1:8200/v1/sys/metrics?format=prometheus | head -n 20
Expected result: the output begins with Prometheus-style comment lines such as # HELP and # TYPE, followed by metric lines (for example vault_core_unsealed), confirming Vault is exposing operational metrics that could be scraped by a monitoring system such as Prometheus.

Finally, run a small repeated-request check to confirm the service stays responsive under light load:

for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do
  curl -s -o /dev/null -w "Request $i: HTTP %{http_code} in %{time_total}s\n" \
    http://127.0.0.1:8200/v1/sys/health
  sleep 1
done
Deliverable: five consecutive lines each showing HTTP 200 with response times consistently under one second, demonstrating that the Ansible-deployed Vault instance is stable and responsive.

Expected Outcomes
A running, unsealed HashiCorp Vault instance deployed entirely through Ansible playbooks and roles
Working policies and secrets demonstrating least-privilege access control, verified with scoped tokens
Confirmation that Vault's health and metrics endpoints report a healthy, responsive service
Troubleshooting
ansible-playbook fails with "vault: command not found" during the post_tasks status check: the vault binary may not be on the PATH used by the Ansible-managed shell environment. Check which vault and confirm the install step in Subtask 1.2 completed before rerunning the playbook.
uri tasks against /v1/sys/init or /v1/sys/unseal return connection refused: Vault may still be starting up or the systemd service failed. Check sudo systemctl status vault --no-pager and sudo journalctl -u vault -n 50 for the actual startup error before re-running the playbook.
Conclusion
In this lab, you used Ansible to fully automate the deployment of HashiCorp Vault, from installing the binary and generating systemd unit and configuration files through initializing, unsealing, and populating Vault with secrets and access policies. You then verified that the developer policy correctly enforced least-privilege access using a scoped token, and confirmed the deployment's operational health by checking Vault's health endpoint, response times, and Prometheus-format metrics. This exercise demonstrated how configuration management tools like Ansible can make secret management infrastructure reproducible, auditable, and testable, which are core practices for defending sensitive credentials in real-world environments. As a next step, consider extending the playbooks to enable Vault's audit logging, integrate a dynamic secrets engine such as the database secrets engine, or automate periodic health checks with a monitoring tool that scrapes the metrics endpoint you validated in Task 3.
